✅ Expected Result Discussion

The diabetes dataset is imbalanced, so:

Accuracy may be misleading

Precision & Recall are more meaningful

KNN performance depends heavily on K, distance metric, and scaling

Typical performance:

Accuracy: 70–80%

Better recall is preferred (missing diabetic patients is costly)

✅ Short Viva Questions & Answers
Q1: Why do we normalize data for KNN?

KNN uses distances; unscaled features dominate the metric unfairly.

Q2: What is the ideal value of K?

Determined through experimentation—generally odd numbers like 5, 7, 9.

Q3: Why is KNN slow for large datasets?

Because it computes distance to all points at prediction time (lazy learner).

Q4: Why use confusion matrix?

To visualize correct and incorrect classifications and assess model reliability.

Q5: Why is diabetes prediction a classification problem?

Because the outcome is categorical (diabetic = 1 or not = 0).
